id: rule-if-maximum
title: Maximum
topic: Conditional
example-of: IF
derived: true

task:
  Use the rule IF to derive this triple. Each branch is proved with the
  rule ASSIGN and the consequence rule.

given:
  Condition B: $x >= y$

pre: true
post: m >= x && m >= y

scheme: IF
  Then branch: {«P» && «x >= y : B»} m := x {«Q»}
  Else branch: {«P» && «!(x >= y) : ¬B»} m := y {«Q»}
  ---
  {«P»} if x >= y then m := x else m := y {«Q»}

program:
  {P}
  if x >= y then {
    {P && x >= y}
    {R1}
    m := x       // then
    {Q}
  } else {
    {P && !(x >= y)}
    {R2}
    m := y       // else
    {Q}
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of m := x
part: Then branch
rule: ASSIGN
focus: then
text:
  We substitute the variable $m$ with $x$ in $Q$.
calc:
  wp(m := x, Q)
  = (m >= x && m >= y)[m <- x]    // Q
  = x >= x && x >= y              // m replaced by x
  = x >= y                        // x >= x is true, and true && A is A
set: R1 = x >= y

step: P and B imply R1
part: Then branch
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P && x >= y, R1
text:
  >>> «P» && «x >= y : B» ==> «R1»
vc: true && x >= y ==> x >= y
why:
  $R1$ is the condition $B$ itself.

step: R2, the weakest precondition of m := y
part: Else branch
rule: ASSIGN
focus: else
text:
  We substitute the variable $m$ with $y$ in $Q$.
calc:
  wp(m := y, Q)
  = (m >= x && m >= y)[m <- y]    // Q
  = y >= x && y >= y              // m replaced by y
  = y >= x                        // y >= y is true, and A && true is A
set: R2 = y >= x

step: P and not B imply R2
part: Else branch
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P && !(x >= y), R2
text:
  >>> «P» && «!(x >= y) : ¬B» ==> «R2»
vc: true && !(x >= y) ==> y >= x
why:
  $!(x >= y)$ means $x < y$, which gives $y >= x$. Both premises hold.
